## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the fundamental understanding of combining data structures to achieve O(1) operations while maintaining an ordering constraint. It evaluates knowledge of hash map + linked list patterns and eviction logic commonly used in caching systems.

## Key Concepts
- HashMap for O(1) key-to-node lookups
- Doubly linked list for maintaining recency order
- Node manipulation for constant-time recency updates
- Edge case handling: missing keys, capacity limits, and duplicate operations

## Code Example
```javascript
class LRUCache {
  constructor(capacity) {
    this.capacity = capacity;
    this.cache = new Map();
    this.head = { prev: null, next: null };
    this.tail = { prev: null, next: null };
    this.head.next = this.tail;
    this.tail.prev = this.head;
  }

  get(key) {
    if (!this.cache.has(key)) return -1;
    const node = this.cache.get(key);
    this._moveToHead(node);
    return node.value;
  }

  put(key, value) {
    if (this.cache.has(key)) {
      const node = this.cache.get(key);
      node.value = value;
      this._moveToHead(node);
    } else {
      const newNode = { key, value, prev: null, next: null };
      this.cache.set(key, newNode);
      this._addToHead(newNode);
      if (this.cache.size > this.capacity) {
        this._removeTail();
      }
    }
  }

  _moveToHead(node) {
    this._removeNode(node);
    this._addToHead(node);
  }

  _addToHead(node) {
    node.prev = this.head;
    node.next = this.head.next;
    this.head.next.prev = node;
    this.head.next = node;
  }

  _removeNode(node) {
    node.prev.next = node.next;
    node.next.prev = node.prev;
  }

  _removeTail() {
    const node = this.tail.prev;
    this._removeNode(node);
    this.cache.delete(node.key);
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests understanding of order statistics in dynamic sets, along with practical implementation details like lazy deletion and handling duplicates.

## Key Concepts

- **Median of a dynamic set**: the middle element (or average of two middle elements) when sorted
- **Two-heap approach**: max-heap for the lower half, min-heap for the upper half
- **Lazy deletion**: defer actual removal until top operations, tracking deletions via a count map
- **Duplicate handling**: maintain frequency counts rather than storing duplicate values multiple times in the heaps

## Code Example

```javascript
class MedianFinder {
  constructor() {
    this.low = new MaxHeap();    // max-heap for lower half
    this.high = new MinHeap();   // min-heap for upper half
    this.delayed = new Map();    // lazy deletion map
  }

  insert(num) {
    if (this.low.isEmpty() || num <= this.low.peek()) {
      this.low.push(num);
    } else {
      this.high.push(num);
    }
    this.rebalance();
  }

  rebalance() {
    if (this.low.size() > this.high.size() + 1) {
      this.high.push(this.low.pop());
    } else if (this.high.size() > this.low.size()) {
      this.low.push(this.high.pop());
    }
  }

  erase(num) {
    this.delayed.set(num, (this.delayed.get(num) || 0) + 1);
    if (num <= this.low.peek()) {
      this.low.deleted++;
    } else {
      this.high.deleted++;
    }
    this.rebalance();
  }

  getMedian() {
    this.prune(this.low);
    this.prune(this.high);
    if (this.low.size() > this.high.size()) {
      return this.low.peek();
    }
    return (this.low.peek() + this.high.peek()) / 2;
  }
}
```

